WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.346
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.450
SPEAKER: To get started with
the Cloud Natural Language API

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.420
for Node.js, you'll need
a GCP project, which

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.720
you can set up in the console.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.260
Next, enable the Google Natural
Language API for that project.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:26.290
Then create a service account
and download the private key

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:27.868
as JSON.

00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:29.410
You're going to use
these credentials

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.670
to access Google Cloud APIs, so
keep this file secure and out

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.700
of your code in public repos.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.340
To access the credentials
from your project,

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.400
go to the terminal and set
an environment variable

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.900
to the path of your
service account.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.050
Prepare your environment
for Node.js development.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.260
Then install the client library.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.580
If you use Cloud
Shell in the console,

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.060
you can skip the step since
the required dependencies

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.470
are already included.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:06.580
Make a new project file
or open an existing file.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:09.430
Import the Google
Cloud client library,

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.230
and then instantiate a client.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:16.990
Provide some text to analyze.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.990
I'm going to go with a
classic example here.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.980
Now create a document which
has a field called content

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.900
set to the text
you are analyzing

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.000
and a field called type,
which, as the name suggests,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.400
is the type of text--

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.680
in our case, plain text.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.340
Call analyzeSentiment,
passing the document.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.160
This function returns a
premise I'm calling results,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.590
which resolves to an array.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.590
The first element of the array
is an object representing

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:48.490
analyze sentiment response.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.720
Analyze sentiment response
has three properties--

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.290
document sentiment,
the overall sentiment

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.030
of the input document--

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.310
language, the
language of the text--

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.800
and sentences, an
array of sentiment

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.450
for each sentence in the text.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.970
Define a constant for the
sentiment of the document.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.580
Log the parameters of
sentiment score and magnitude.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.870
Score is a sentiment
score between negative 1

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.000
for negative sentiment
and positive 1

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.290
for positive sentiment.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.090
Magnitude is a number
between 0 and infinity

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:24.820
which represents the
absolute magnitude

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:29.260
of the sentiment regardless
of positive or negative score.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:33.830
Finally, include a catch
block to handle any errors.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:38.030
Save this file and run the code.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:39.580
If everything
worked properly, you

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.390
should see the original
text, a sentiment score,

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.380
and a magnitude in the terminal.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:48.960
If not, take a look back at
the code for any syntax errors.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.510
Congratulations, you've
just sent your first request

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.670
to the Natural Language API.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.880
But there is more that the Cloud
Natural Language API can do,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.860
like analyzing syntax
and annotating text.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.140
To find out more about
the client libraries,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:06.410
consult natural language basics,
or work through this sentiment

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:10.910
analysis tutorial, check
out the Cloud documentation.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

